the government in Bangladesh is  threatening to send tens of thousands of  refugees to a remote island by force  despite warnings from environmentalists  and rights groups accommodation on the  island abbis and char has been specially  constructed for the refugees the  government says it will offer a new  start for the reinjure but the low-lying  island in the bay of bengal is  vulnerable to erosion and flooding and  critics have condemned the plan  our correspondent Naomi Conrad was  granted access to the site in the  world's largest refugee camp in southern  Bangladesh fear is rife two years ago  the government welcomed the influx of  Ranga fleeing Myanmar today it is  planning to send tens of thousands of  them to an island in an area prone to  cyclones we don't want to go to bath and  char because it gets flooded and then  people will die our children will be  drowned for them it's another trap where  they'll lose their lives if we go there  we'll die help us to stay here we lost  our parents and came here to save our  lives don't send us to pass on Chau just  kill us here no Western journalists have  been granted access to the island until  now DW was taken to bazan char by  Bangladesh Navy which is overseeing the  construction works throughout our stay  we were accompanied by naval officers  they took us three hours to reach Bazin  char on a naval ship the sea was choppy  but relatively calm for this time of the  year  what is the longest period that you  think chips might not be able to reach  the island and didn't work really bad  weather conditions if the cyclonic storm  warning that maybe one or two days or  three days maximum not more than that  and we have no worker we have for the  Navy ship we have never stopped nobody  will be evacuated from this island that  is because the Navy says they have built  sufficient protection against floods and  erosion including a three meter high  embankment inside their space for  100,000 row hangers housed in clusters  of identical shelters human rights  organizations fear that refugees may be  contained on the remote island for years  sometimes this this island is described  as a jail does that offend you  yes the question is the people who makes  comment like floating island unsafe  island yes they don't understand the  island they didn't see the island and  they don't know about the hot facility  we are providing the islands 120 cyclone  shelters can be used as hospitals  primary schools and community centers to  provide security there will be police  posts and cameras monitoring the island  we will provide something to the RO  hangar they will remember it for their  life and is in the crisis we help them  if they come here it is paradise for  them is the no doubt about them from  what we've seen conditions here do seem  to be better than in [ __ ] bizarre but  questions remain around what daily life  might be like will there be secondary  education for young people what will  they do all day can the refugees earn  their own living and will they be able  to leave the island without a special  permit and of course what happens here  when a cyclone hits  bazan Shah didn't exist 20 years ago  islands in the Bay of Bengal are formed  by sediments and make up an  ever-changing ecosystem we showed the  footage we took to an expert in sea  erosions Sancho a direction of boggling  obstacle nodule it's a geographically  fidgel so it can be eroded it can be  washed because a sandy island orchards  at least two to three beakers to be  permanent without an embankment buzz and  char would be too dangerous for  habitation experts DW contacted  disagreed whether the barrier is high  enough but the government seems adamant  to relocate row hangar to the island  they may port them with that we don't  have enough space here so we will for  them if they are not really will force  them once the decision has been taken  refugees could be relocated in a matter  of weeks even against their will and EWS  Naomi Conrad who filed that report is  with us here in the studio great to see  you now me so what's your impression of  the camp when you were there were you're  not thinking well this is going to be a  lot better for the refugees than Cox's  Bazar where hundreds of thousands are  living in really difficult conditions  well if you look at the settlement  itself yes it's it's an improvement the  people would have more space the rooms  that area the the camps and Cox's Bazar  stiflingly hot in the summer I mean I  went inside some of the hearts it's  really difficult to spend for me to even  just spend half an hour for an interview  inside these hurts so if you look at the  settlement itself yes the infrastructure  is a lot better there's security  the roads are secured but then you have  to look at the island itself and the  questions that you know we try to dance  and the report of whether this island is  actually safe whether the flood  embankment is high enough so the  settlement itself looks a lot better but  the bigger questions around the island  and also whether were the refugees would  be allowed off the island with the they  would have free movement  there's other questions now you spoke  with me on numerous foreign minister  about the relocation plans he suggested  that the revenger could be moved to the  island even if the UN objects let's  listen to what he had to say what is the  most extreme step you could take if the  UN disagrees would you kick the UN  agencies out induce will if that is  necessary will do so to be clear if they  don't support your plan of buzzin shy  you will tell them to we have to make a  decision and we'll talk to them and see  you so it sounds like Bangladesh is  government is absolutely determined to  send the refugees to this island  what is the UN saying about it yes I  mean Bangladesh built this settlement  for the Ringo they spent a lot of money  on it so of course they want to move the  ringer there but it's difficult because  the yearn I mean the agencies they're  footing the bill of this multi-million  dollar humanitarian effort I at Cox's  Bazar so they they depend on  international donors to feed the  refugees to provide schooling for the  refugees to you know provide all the  basic health care all the basic  necessities that the refugees need so it  would be incredibly difficult to to  force the refugees to leave against the  against the the UN's wishes it's a  difficult situation diplomatically very  sensitive at this point apparently a  number of the refugees really are saying  that they do not want to go the island  they are afraid to go there for the  reasons that you mentioned if not enough  volunteers are found among the refugees  how would the government go about  forcing them together  yes it's very difficult to imagine them  sending in tanks or soldiers of course  but what they seem to be doing right now  and that this just came a couple of days  ago is that they want to make the living  conditions or difficult in Cox's Bazar  so we just heard that they've they're  forcing the telecommunications providers  to turn off the internet not to sell SIM  cards to hinga anymore  which would basically cut them off from  any kind of communication so there seems  to be that kind of pressure you know  putting pressure on the refugees to make  the living conditions even harder in  Cox's Bazar which may then lead  a certain number of ringer to maybe sign  up voluntarily to move to this island  which then suddenly looks maybe more  attractive than it does now very quickly  how soon do you think we could see those  refugees moved to the army well in in  Dhaka there was talk of after the  monsoon so in that coming weeks  okay Naomi thank you so much DW  correspondent Naomi Conrad

